{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And all the congregation of the children of Israel went out.</b> Meaning, that they all went out together with alacrity to bring, and not one of them lingered behind another." "<b>From before Moses.</b> Meaning, from before him — before he would give them permission to take their leave from before him. And even though we say in Yoma (53a) that a disciple has no permission to take his leave from before his master until the master gives him permission, Israel reasoned that when he said to them ״קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה״ etc. and finished his words, it was as though he had said to them: You may go. Or it is possible that love of the matter disrupted the order of things. Further, it appears that they intended to precede him, so that Moses would not go and preempt them by bringing the entire need from his own property — for he was very wealthy, and the passion to perform the commandments of HaShem is great. Therefore they went out מִלְּפָנָיו — meaning, in the manner of the verse's saying (Ruth 4:7) ״וְזֹאת לְפָנִים״ — before him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:52.712901329Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}